    {
      "id": "sol.hmmt-nov-guts-2011-p7.hmmt",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "16 We note that \\(54000=2^{4} \\times 3^{3} \\times 5^{3}\\). Hence, we must have \\(a=2^{a_{1}} 3^{a_{2}} 5^{a_{3}}, b=2^{b_{1}} 3^{b_{2}} 5^{b_{3}}\\), \\(c=2^{c_{1}} 3^{c_{2}} 5^{c_{3}}\\). We look at each prime factor individually:\n\n- \\(4 a_{1}+2 b_{1}+c_{1}=4\\) gives 4 solutions: \\((1,0,0),(0,2,0),(0,1,2),(0,0,4)\\)\n- \\(4 a_{2}+2 b_{2}+c_{2}=3\\) and \\(4 a_{3}+2 b_{3}+c_{3}=3\\) each give 2 solutions: \\((0,1,1),(0,1,3)\\).\n\nHence, we have a total of \\(4 \\times 2 \\times 2=16\\) solutions.",
